<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Durban</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Port Natal, eThekwini</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Durban is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>'s busiest ports, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> coast. British traders founded Port Natal there in 1824, and it was renamed in 1835 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-durban">Sir Benjamin D'Urban</a>, governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>. It grew as the port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colony-of-natal">Colony of Natal</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations nearby, and became home to a large community of Indian origin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/durban.jpg" alt="Durban" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dennis Sylvester Hurd · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Durban_from_the_Balcony_(Dennis_Sylvester_Hurd)_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Durban lies on the Natal Bay of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. North of the harbour and city centre is the mouth of the Umgeni River, and the flat city centre rises to the hills of the Berea on the west, while the Bluff runs along the coast to the south. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> name, eThekwini, comes from itheku, meaning bay or lagoon. The city has a humid subtropical climate with hot, wet summers and mild, dry winters. Its natural harbour is one of the few between Port Elizabeth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maputo">Maputo</a>, and it became a busy port of call for ship repairs after the port opened in the 1840s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological evidence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a> suggests that hunter-gatherers lived in the area from about 100,000 years ago, until agro-pastoralists from the north gradually absorbed them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> sailed along the coast at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1497 and named it Natal, Portuguese for Christmas. In 1686 survivors of the wrecked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> ship Stavenisse were taken in by the Abambo people, a group of the Hlubi.</p><p>In 1823 James Saunders King and Lieutenant Francis George Farewell, former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> officers, sheltered from a storm in the Bay of Natal. In 1824 Farewell, with the trading company J. R. Thompson and Co. and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-francis-fynn">Henry Francis Fynn</a>, set up a trading station at the bay and opened trade with Shaka, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> king, who granted them land around it. The settlement was named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-durban">Sir Benjamin D'Urban</a> in 1835 and became a borough in 1854. The Natal Railway Company opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s first operating steam railway, between the Point and the town, in 1860, and in the same year indentured labourers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> began to arrive.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a> founded the Natalia Republic in 1839, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietermaritzburg">Pietermaritzburg</a> as its capital. To keep Port Natal, Governor George Thomas Napier sent a force under Captain Thomas Charlton Smith, which built a fortified camp in May 1842. After its attack on a camp at Congella failed, the Voortrekkers besieged it. The trader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dick-king">Dick King</a> and his servant Ndongeni rode to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makhanda">Grahamstown</a> in 14 days to fetch reinforcements, and the siege was lifted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed the republic in 1843. Natal became a province of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> in 1910, and Durban received city status in 1935.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Durban is the third most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a>, and its harbour is the busiest port in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sub-saharan-africa">sub-Saharan Africa</a>. It is the seat of the eThekwini Metropolitan Municipality, formed in 1999. Its people include large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>, Indian, White and Coloured communities, and the Indian community has influenced its cuisine, culture and religion. Its architecture runs from Victorian to contemporary, with Art Deco on many buildings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Durban” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Then the gold of the Witwatersrand shifted the economic centre of South Africa from the Cape to the Transvaal, drew railways from Cape Town, <b>Durban</b> and Delagoa Bay to the Rand, and created Johannesburg, which became the country’s largest city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Rivalry among the four colonies over railways and customs duties, together with a wish among white politicians to settle ‘native policy’ on a common footing, led to the National Convention of 1908–1909, which met in <b>Durban</b>, Cape Town and Bloemfontein.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Trade with Delagoa Bay and, from 1824, with British traders such as Francis Farewell and Henry Francis Fynn at <b>Port Natal</b> brought beads, brass and cloth in exchange for ivory and cattle.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Resistance revived in the 1970s: Black Consciousness inspired a new generation, strikes in <b>Durban</b> in 1973 brought out tens of thousands of workers, and on 16 June 1976 police in Soweto fired on schoolchildren marching against the use of Afrikaans in their schools, killing Hector Pieterson among others.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Through them the movement was involved in staging the large-scale protests and workers' strikes that gripped the nation in 1972 and 1973, especially in <b>Durban</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/black-consciousness-movement">Black Consciousness Movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The new union, launched in <b>Durban</b> on 9 July 2002, gave itself the right to intervene in a member state in grave circumstances, namely war crimes, genocide and crimes against humanity, a provision widely seen as a response to the failure to stop the genocide in Rwanda in…</blockquote><a href="/movement/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism · Outcomes</a></li></ul></section>
